```c
#include <stddef.h>

char *reverse_string(char *s) {
    if (s == NULL) {
        return NULL;
    }
    size_t left = 0;
    size_t right = 0;
    while (s[right] != '\0') {
        right++;
    }
    if (right == 0) {
        return s;
    }
    right--;
    while (left < right) {
        char tmp = s[left];
        s[left] = s[right];
        s[right] = tmp;
        left++;
        right--;
    }
    return s;
}
```

The function walks to the terminator to find the length, then swaps from both ends inward. `left < right` stops correctly for both even and odd lengths, so the middle character of an odd-length string is left untouched. NULL returns NULL rather than crashing, and an empty string returns immediately.

