Empty input prints nothing and exits 0. Ties on frequency are resolved by ascending key so the output is deterministic.

```rust
fn is_balanced(input: &str) -> bool {
    let mut stack: Vec<char> = Vec::new();
    for ch in input.chars() {
        match ch {
            '(' | '[' | '{' => stack.push(ch),
            ')' | ']' | '}' => {
                let expected = match ch {
                    ')' => '(',
                    ']' => '[',
                    _ => '{',
                };
                if stack.pop() != Some(expected) {
                    return false;
                }
            }
            _ => {}
        }
    }
    stack.is_empty()
}

fn main() {
    let cases = ["(a[b]{c})", "([)]", "", "(((", "a)b", "[{()}]}<"];
    for case in cases {
        println!("{:?} -> {}", case, is_balanced(case));
    }
}
```

Output: `"(a[b]{c})" -> true`, `"([)]" -> false`, `"" -> true`, `"(((" -> false`, `"a)b" -> false`, `"[{()}]}<" -> true` (the trailing `<` is ignored as a non-bracket). A mismatched closer is rejected immediately, and any leftover opener at the end fails the `is_empty` check.

