```rust
fn merge_sorted<T: Ord + Clone>(a: &[T], b: &[T]) -> Vec<T> {
    let mut out = Vec::with_capacity(a.len() + b.len());
    let (mut i, mut j) = (0, 0);

    while i < a.len() && j < b.len() {
        if a[i] <= b[j] {
            out.push(a[i].clone());
            i += 1;
        } else {
            out.push(b[j].clone());
            j += 1;
        }
    }
    out.extend_from_slice(&a[i..]);
    out.extend_from_slice(&b[j..]);
    out
}

fn main() {
    let a = [1, 3, 5, 7];
    let b = [2, 4, 6, 8, 10];
    assert_eq!(merge_sorted(&a, &b), vec![1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8, 10]);

    let empty: [i32; 0] = [];
    assert_eq!(merge_sorted(&empty, &b), b.to_vec());
    assert_eq!(merge_sorted(&a, &empty), a.to_vec());
    assert_eq!(merge_sorted(&empty, &empty), Vec::<i32>::new());

    println!("all assertions passed");
}
```
After the main loop, whichever slice still has elements is appended whole, which is safe because each input is already sorted. The empty-slice cases fall out for free since the corresponding index starts at `len` and the `extend_from_slice` copies nothing. The bound is `Ord + Clone`; if you want to avoid cloning you could consume owned `Vec`s instead.

