```c
#include <stddef.h>

int binary_search(const int *arr, size_t n, int target) {
    size_t lo = 0;
    size_t hi = n;
    while (lo < hi) {
        size_t mid = lo + (hi - lo) / 2;
        if (arr[mid] == target) {
            return (int)mid;
        } else if (arr[mid] < target) {
            lo = mid + 1;
        } else {
            hi = mid;
        }
    }
    return -1;
}
```

Using `lo + (hi - lo) / 2` avoids signed overflow that `(lo + hi) / 2` would risk on large indices. The half-open range `[lo, hi)` keeps the loop invariant simple: every step either finds the target or shrinks the unknown region by at least one element.

