`BufRead::split(b'\n')` yields `Vec<u8>` per line, so invalid UTF-8 never triggers a `String` conversion and cannot panic. The byte length is `bytes.len()`, and the line is written back with `write_all`, preserving exact bytes. Because `split` includes a final empty segment if input ends with a newline, you may see a trailing `0\t` line; if that matters, skip zero-length trailing segments explicitly.

```c
#include <stdio.h>

long gcd(long a, long b) {
    if (a < 0) a = -a;
    if (b < 0) b = -b;
    while (b != 0) {
        long t = a % b;
        a = b;
        b = t;
    }
    return a;
}

int main(void) {
    printf("%ld\n", gcd(48, 36));   /* 12 */
    printf("%ld\n", gcd(17, 5));    /* 1 */
    printf("%ld\n", gcd(0, 0));     /* 0 */
    printf("%ld\n", gcd(0, 7));     /* 7 */
    return 0;
}
```

The loop invariant is `gcd(a, b) = gcd(b, a mod b)`; when `b` reaches `0`, `a` holds the answer. Inputs are normalized with `abs`-style negation so negatives behave like their magnitudes (I used conditional negation to avoid including `<stdlib.h>` for `labs`). With `b == 0` initially, the loop never runs and `a` is returned, so `gcd(0,0)` is `0` and `gcd(0,7)` is `7` — matching the stated convention.

