```rust
pub fn reverse_i32(mut n: i32) -> Option<i32> {
    let mut result: i32 = 0;
    while n != 0 {
        let digit = n % 10;
        n /= 10;
        result = result.checked_mul(10)?.checked_add(digit)?;
    }
    Some(result)
}

fn main() {
    assert_eq!(reverse_i32(123), Some(321));
    assert_eq!(reverse_i32(-456), Some(-654));
    assert_eq!(reverse_i32(0), Some(0));
    assert_eq!(reverse_i32(1534236469), None); // 9646324351 overflows i32
    assert_eq!(reverse_i32(i32::MIN), None);
    println!("ok");
}
```
`checked_mul` and `checked_add` return `None` the moment the next accumulated value would exceed `i32` bounds, so overflow is detected at the exact step it happens instead of relying on a wider type. Negative numbers work because Rust's `%` keeps the sign of the dividend.

```c
#include <errno.h>
#include <limits.h>
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>

int parse_int(const char *s, int *out) {
    if (s == NULL || *s == '\0') return -1;
    errno = 0;
    char *end = NULL;
    long v = strtol(s, &end, 10);
    if (end == s || *end != '\0') return -1;      /* no digits or trailing junk */
    if (errno == ERANGE || v < INT_MIN || v > INT_MAX) return -1; /* overflow */
    *out = (int)v;
    return 0;
}

int main(void) {
    int v;
    if (parse_int("42", &v) != 0 || v != 42) return 1;
    if (parse_int("-7", &v) != 0 || v != -7) return 1;
    if (parse_int("", &v) == 0) return 1;
    if (parse_int("12x", &v) == 0) return 1;
    if (parse_int("9999999999999999999", &v) == 0) return 1;
    puts("ok");
    return 0;
}
```
`strtol` sets `errno` to `ERANGE` on overflow and points `end` at the first unparsed character. Checking `end == s` catches input with no digits at all, `*end != '\0'` rejects trailing junk, and the explicit `INT_MIN`/`INT_MAX` range check catches values that fit in `long` but not `int`.

