    for (size_t i = 0; i < len / 2; ++i) {
        char tmp = s[i];
        s[i] = s[len - 1 - i];
        s[len - 1 - i] = tmp;
    }
}

int main(void) {
    char a[] = "hello";
    char b[] = "";
    char c[] = "a";
    reverse_in_place(a);
    reverse_in_place(b);
    reverse_in_place(c);
    printf("%s\n%s\n%s\n", a, b, c);
    return 0;
}
```

It swaps symmetric characters up to the midpoint, so odd and even lengths both work, and the empty-string and single-character cases are no-ops. The `NULL` guard keeps it from dereferencing a null pointer.

