```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <limits>
#include <stdexcept>

unsigned long long fib(int n) {
    if (n < 0) throw std::invalid_argument("n must be >= 0");
    unsigned long long a = 0, b = 1;
    for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {
        if (a > std::numeric_limits<unsigned long long>::max() - b)
            throw std::overflow_error("fib overflow");
        unsigned long long next = a + b;
        a = b;
        b = next;
    }
    return a;
}

int main() {
    std::cout << fib(0) << '\n';
    std::cout << fib(10) << '\n';
    std::cout << fib(50) << '\n';
    try {
        std::cout << fib(100) << '\n';
    } catch (const std::overflow_error& e) {
        std::cout << "overflow at n=100\n";
    }
    return 0;
}
```

`a` and `b` are consecutive Fibonacci numbers; each iteration advances both without recursion or extra memory. Before adding, the code checks whether `a + b` would exceed `ULLONG_MAX` using subtraction to avoid a wrap-around in the check itself. `fib(0)` returns 0 correctly because the return is `a`, which stays 0 when the loop body never runs.

```c
#include <ctype.h>
#include <string.h>

char *trim_in_place(char *s) {
    if (s == NULL) return NULL;
    while (*s && isspace((unsigned char)*s)) s++;
    size_t len = strlen(s);
    while (len > 0 && isspace((unsigned char)s[len - 1])) {
        s[--len] = '\0';
    }
    return s;
}
```

Leading spaces are skipped by advancing the pointer, so the returned pointer is the new start and the caller must use the return value, not the original. Trailing spaces are removed from the end by writing `\0`. Casting to `unsigned char` before `isspace` avoids undefined behaviour on negative `char` values. A NULL pointer is returned unchanged instead of crashing, and an all-whitespace string trims to an empty string.

