def longest_palindrome(s: str) -> str:
    """Return the longest palindromic substring of s (expand around center)."""
    if not s:
        return ""

    def expand(left: int, right: int) -> tuple[int, int]:
        while left >= 0 and right < len(s) and s[left] == s[right]:
            left -= 1
            right += 1
        return left + 1, right  # inclusive start, exclusive end

    best_start, best_end = 0, 0
    for i in range(len(s)):
        for lo, hi in (expand(i, i), expand(i, i + 1)):
            if hi - lo > best_end - best_start:
                best_start, best_end = lo, hi
    return s[best_start:best_end]


if __name__ == "__main__":
    cases = ["babad", "cbbd", "a", "", "forgeeksskeegfor", "abacaba", ""]
    for text in cases:
        print(f"{text!r} -> {longest_palindrome(text)!r}")
```

Expansion from each center is O(n) per center, so O(n²) time and O(1) extra space. The two-center loop covers odd and even-length palindromes. Empty input returns an empty string rather than raising.

