#include <stddef.h>
#include <string.h>

void reverse_string(char *s) {
    if (s == NULL) {
        return;
    }
    size_t len = strlen(s);
    if (len < 2) {
        return;
    }
    size_t i = 0;
    size_t j = len - 1;
    while (i < j) {
        char tmp = s[i];
        s[i] = s[j];
        s[j] = tmp;
        i++;
        j--;
    }
}
```

The function uses two indices converging from both ends. It checks for `NULL` and skips any string of length 0 or 1. Since it operates on raw bytes, multi-byte UTF-8 sequences would be reversed bytewise, so it is safe only for ASCII input unless you reverse by codepoint instead.

