```rust
fn merge_sorted<T: Ord + Clone>(a: &[T], b: &[T]) -> Vec<T> {
    let mut out = Vec::with_capacity(a.len() + b.len());
    let (mut i, mut j) = (0, 0);
    while i < a.len() && j < b.len() {
        if a[i] <= b[j] {
            out.push(a[i].clone());
            i += 1;
        } else {
            out.push(b[j].clone());
            j += 1;
        }
    }
    out.extend_from_slice(&a[i..]);
    out.extend_from_slice(&b[j..]);
    out
}

fn main() {
    assert_eq!(merge_sorted(&[1, 3, 5], &[2, 4, 6]), vec![1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6]);
    assert_eq!(merge_sorted::<i32>(&[], &[1, 2]), vec![1, 2]);
    assert_eq!(merge_sorted(&[9], &[]), vec![9]);
    println!("ok");
}
```

The `<=` comparison keeps the merge stable when equal elements appear. After the loop, at least one slice is exhausted, so the two `extend_from_slice` calls append the leftovers in order. `with_capacity` avoids reallocations, and the `T: Ord + Clone` bound keeps it generic over any ordered, cloneable type.

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <limits.h>

int fib(unsigned n, unsigned long long *out) {
    unsigned long long a = 0, b = 1;
    for (unsigned i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        if (a > ULLONG_MAX - b) {
            return -1;
        }
        unsigned long long next = a + b;
        a = b;
        b = next;
    }
    *out = a;
    return 0;
}

int main(void) {
    unsigned long long v;
    if (fib(10, &v) != 0) return 1;
    printf("fib(10) = %llu\n", v);
    if (fib(100, &v) == 0) printf("fib(100) = %llu\n", v);
    return 0;
}
```

The check `a > ULLONG_MAX - b` runs before the addition so the sum never wraps. On overflow the function returns `-1` and leaves `*out` untouched, so the caller can distinguish failure. `fib(10)` is 55 and `fib(100)` fits comfortably in 64 bits.

