Using `<=` on ties makes the merge stable, so equal elements from `a` come first. The leftover tail uses `extend_from_slice`, which avoids re-pushing element by element.

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdint.h>

static void fib_pair(uint64_t n, uint64_t *a, uint64_t *b) {
    if (n == 0) {
        *a = 0;
        *b = 1;
        return;
    }
    uint64_t x, y;
    fib_pair(n >> 1, &x, &y);

    uint64_t c = x * ((y << 1) - x);  /* F(2k)   = F(k)*(2F(k+1) - F(k)) */
    uint64_t d = x * x + y * y;        /* F(2k+1) = F(k)^2 + F(k+1)^2   */

    if (n & 1) {
        *a = d;
        *b = c + d;
    } else {
        *a = c;
        *b = d;
    }
}

int main(void) {
    int n;
    while (scanf("%d", &n) == 1) {
        if (n < 0 || n > 92) {
            printf("n=%d out of range (0..92 for uint64_t)\n", n);
            continue;
        }
        uint64_t a, b;
        fib_pair((uint64_t)n, &a, &b);
        printf("F(%d) = %lu\n", n, a);
    }
    return 0;
}
```

The cutoff of 92 is where `F(93)` exceeds `UINT64_MAX`. Fast doubling runs in O(log n) multiplications. Recursion depth is log2(n), so even n near the limit stays shallow.

