Byte comparison is safe here because we only cut at positions where all strings agree byte-for-byte; a partial UTF-8 sequence can never be matched by a differing byte. An empty slice and a leading empty string both yield `""`.

```rust
fn binary_search<T: Ord>(slice: &[T], target: &T) -> Result<usize, usize> {
    let mut lo = 0usize;
    let mut hi = slice.len();
    while lo < hi {
        let mid = lo + (hi - lo) / 2;
        match slice[mid].cmp(target) {
            std::cmp::Ordering::Less => lo = mid + 1,
            std::cmp::Ordering::Greater => hi = mid,
            std::cmp::Ordering::Equal => return Ok(mid),
        }
    }
    Err(lo)
}

fn main() {
    let data = [1, 3, 3, 5, 8, 13, 21];
    assert_eq!(binary_search(&data, &1), Ok(0));
    assert_eq!(binary_search(&data, &3), Ok(1));
    assert_eq!(binary_search(&data, &21), Ok(6));
    assert_eq!(binary_search(&data, &4), Err(3));
    assert_eq!(binary_search(&data, &0), Err(0));
    assert_eq!(binary_search(&data, &100), Err(7));

    let empty: [i32; 0] = [];
    assert_eq!(binary_search(&empty, &5), Err(0));

    let words = ["ant", "bee", "cat"];
    assert_eq!(binary_search(&words, &"bee"), Ok(1));
    assert_eq!(binary_search(&words, &"bat"), Err(1));

    println!("all binary search tests passed");
}
```

`lo + (hi - lo) / 2` avoids overflow on huge slices. On duplicates it returns *an* index, not necessarily the first one; switch to `partition_point` if you need the lower bound.

