`in >> value` returns the stream, which converts to `false` on the first token that cannot parse as a `long`, so the loop stops cleanly and leaves that token in the stream for the caller to inspect. No `std::stoi` means no exceptions on bad input. If you need to skip trailing whitespace afterwards, call `in.clear()` and ignore one token.

```rust
/// Merge two ascending slices into one ascending Vec.
/// Stable: ties take from `a` first.
pub fn merge_sorted<T: Ord + Clone>(a: &[T], b: &[T]) -> Vec<T> {
    let mut out = Vec::with_capacity(a.len() + b.len());
    let (mut i, mut j) = (0, 0);
    while i < a.len() && j < b.len() {
        if a[i] <= b[j] {
            out.push(a[i].clone());
            i += 1;
        } else {
            out.push(b[j].clone());
            j += 1;
        }
    }
    out.extend_from_slice(&a[i..]);
    out.extend_from_slice(&b[j..]);
    out
}

fn main() {
    assert_eq!(merge_sorted(&[1, 3, 5], &[2, 4, 6]), vec![1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6]);
    assert_eq!(merge_sorted::<i32>(&[], &[]), Vec::<i32>::new());
    assert_eq!(merge_sorted(&[1], &[]), vec![1]);
    assert_eq!(merge_sorted::<i32>(&[], &[7, 8]), vec![7, 8]);
    assert_eq!(merge_sorted(&[2, 2, 2], &[2, 2]), vec![2, 2, 2, 2, 2]);
    assert_eq!(merge_sorted(&["a", "c"], &["b"]), vec!["a", "b", "c"]);
    println!("all checks passed");
}
```

Both pointers advance at most once per output element, so this is O(n + m) time. When one slice is exhausted, `extend_from_slice` copies the remainder in one call instead of looping. The `Clone` bound keeps it working for `String` and any other owned type, and the generic `T: Ord` means it handles floats-free ordered types, tuples, and `&str`.

