Opening with `std::ios::binary` prevents the C++ runtime from translating `\r\n` on Windows, which would change the bytes and the hash. The loop reads 64 KiB at a time; `gcount()` returns however many bytes actually arrived on the final short read, and the vector is resized down to that count so the hash only sees real data. The FNV-1a constants are the standard 32-bit ones: offset basis `2166136261u` and prime `16777619u`, with xor before multiply. Passing `hash` back in each call lets the chunks chain into one digest. Output is zero-padded to eight hex digits so short hashes stay aligned.

```python
def longest_palindrome(s: str) -> str:
    if not s:
        return ""

    best_start, best_end = 0, 1

    def expand(left: int, right: int):
        while left >= 0 and right < len(s) and s[left] == s[right]:
            left -= 1
            right += 1
        return left + 1, right  # half-open range

    for i in range(len(s)):
        start, end = expand(i, i)       # odd length
        if end - start > best_end - best_start:
            best_start, best_end = start, end
        start, end = expand(i, i + 1)   # even length
        if end - start > best_end - best_start:
            best_start, best_end = start, end

    return s[best_start:best_end]


if __name__ == "__main__":
    cases = ["", "a", "babad", "cbbd", "forgeeksskeegfor", "😀ab😀"]
    for c in cases:
        print(repr(c), "->", repr(longest_palindrome(c)))
```

Expand around each of the `2n-1` centers in O(n) per center, O(n²) overall. The empty string returns `""`; single characters return themselves; the emoji is one code point in Python 3, so `"😀ab😀"` correctly yields `"😀ab😀"`.

