```cpp
#include <cstdint>
#include <iostream>

int popcount(uint64_t n) {
    int count = 0;
    while (n) {
        n &= (n - 1);   // drops the lowest set bit
        ++count;
    }
    return count;
}

int main() {
    const uint64_t values[] = {
        0ULL,
        1ULL,
        0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFULL,
        0x8000000000000001ULL,
        0x00000000FFFFFFFFULL,
    };
    for (uint64_t v : values) {
        std::cout << "0x" << std::hex << v << std::dec
                  << " -> " << popcount(v) << '\n';
    }
    return 0;
}
```

Subtracting one flips the lowest set bit to 0 and every bit below it to 1; ANDing with the original clears exactly that lowest set bit, so the loop runs once per set bit rather than once per bit. `0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF` reports 64, `0x8000000000000001` reports 2, and 0 reports 0. The argument is 64-bit, so `0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFULL` does not overflow the parameter.

