fn main() {
    for n in [0u32, 1, 10, 50, 92, 93, 100] {
        match fib(n) {
            Some(v) => println!("fib({n}) = {v}"),
            None => println!("fib({n}) overflows u64"),
        }
    }
}
```

`checked_add` returns `None` the moment the next value would exceed `u64::MAX`, so `fib(93)` is the first overflow and the `?` propagates it cleanly. Starting from `(0, 1)` and looping `n` times makes `fib(0) == 0` and `fib(1) == 1` without a special case. The algorithm is O(n) time and O(1) space. Output shows values up to `fib(92) = 7540113804746346429`.

