```cpp
#include <cassert>
#include <cstddef>
#include <iostream>

struct Node {
    int value;
    Node* next;
    explicit Node(int v) : value(v), next(nullptr) {}
};

// Reverse in place and return the new head.
Node* reverse(Node* head) {
    Node* prev = nullptr;
    Node* curr = head;
    while (curr != nullptr) {
        Node* next = curr->next;  // save the rest of the list
        curr->next = prev;        // flip the link backwards
        prev = curr;              // advance prev to the node we just fixed
        curr = next;              // advance curr to the saved node
    }
    return prev;  // prev is the new head; nullptr for an empty list
}

int main() {
    // Empty list stays empty.
    assert(reverse(nullptr) == nullptr);

    // Build 1 -> 2 -> 3 -> 4.
    Node* head = new Node(1);
    head->next = new Node(2);
    head->next->next = new Node(3);
    head->next->next->next = new Node(4);

    head = reverse(head);

    int expected = 4;
    for (Node* n = head; n != nullptr; n = n->next) {
        assert(n->value == expected--);
    }

    // Free the list.
    while (head != nullptr) {
        Node* dead = head;
        head = head->next;
        delete dead;
    }

    std::cout << "reversed correctly\n";
    return 0;
}
```

Each iteration saves `curr->next` before overwriting it, so nothing is lost. `prev` trails one node behind and becomes the new head when `curr` walks off the end. Time is O(n), space is O(1). The empty-list case returns `nullptr` naturally because the loop never runs.

```cpp
#include <cassert>
#include <cstdlib>
#include <iostream>

// Iterative Euclid. Works for negatives by taking absolute values first.
long long gcd(long long a, long long b) {
    a = std::llabs(a);
    b = std::llabs(b);
    while (b != 0) {
        long long t = a % b;
        a = b;
        b = t;
    }
    return a;  // gcd(0, 0) == 0 by convention
}

int main() {
    assert(gcd(48, 18) == 6);
    assert(gcd(18, 48) == 6);
    assert(gcd(-48, 18) == 6);
    assert(gcd(48, -18) == 6);
    assert(gcd(-48, -18) == 6);
    assert(gcd(0, 5) == 5);
    assert(gcd(5, 0) == 5);
    assert(gcd(0, 0) == 0);
    assert(gcd(7, 13) == 1);

    std::cout << "gcd checks passed\n";
    return 0;
}
```

`std::llabs` strips the sign so the remainder loop only sees non-negative values; otherwise `a % b` with a negative `a` yields a negative remainder and the loop terminates at a wrong value. `gcd(0, 0)` returns `0`, which is the conventional identity with no common divisors. The function is O(log min(|a|, |b|)) because each step strictly shrinks the second argument.

```rust
use std::collections::HashMap;

/// Returns the most common word, lowercased, or None if there are no words.
/// Ties are broken by first appearance.
pub fn most_common_word(text: &str) -> Option<String> {
    let mut counts: HashMap<String, (usize, usize)> = HashMap::new();
    let mut order = 0usize;

    for raw in text.split_whitespace() {
        let word: String = raw
            .chars()
            .filter(|c| c.is_alphanumeric())
            .flat_map(|c| c.to_lowercase())
            .collect();

        if word.is_empty() {
            continue;
        }

        let entry = counts.entry(word).or_insert((0, order));
        entry.0 += 1;
        order += 1;
    }

    // max_by_key keeps the last max element on ties, so compare (count, -first_seen).
    counts
        .into_iter()
        .max_by_key(|(_, (count, first))| (*count, usize::MAX - *first))
        .map(|(word, _)| word)
}

fn main() {
    assert_eq!(most_common_word(""), None);
    assert_eq!(most_common_word("   "), None);
    assert_eq!(most_common_word("the cat and the hat"), Some("the".to_string()));
    assert_eq!(most_common_word("One one ONE two"), Some("one".to_string()));
    assert_eq!(most_common_word("hello, world! hello"), Some("hello".to_string()));

    println!("word count checks passed");
}
```

The `HashMap` maps each normalized word to `(count, first_seen_index)`. Filtering through `char::is_alphanumeric` removes punctuation while keeping unicode letters, and `to_lowercase` handles multi-char case folding like `İ`. The tie-break uses `usize::MAX - first` inside `max_by_key` because that function keeps the earlier of two equal elements, and we want the word that appeared first in the text.

```rust
fn is_palindrome(text: &str) -> bool {
    let normalized: Vec<char> = text
        .chars()
        .filter(|c| c.is_alphanumeric())
        .flat_map(|c| c.to_lowercase())
        .collect();

    let mut left = 0usize;
    let mut right = normalized.len().saturating_sub(1);

    // Empty and single-character inputs are palindromes.
    if normalized.is_empty() {
        return true;
    }

    while left < right {
        if normalized[left] != normalized[right] {
            return false;
        }
        left += 1;
        right -= 1;
    }
    true
}

fn main() {
    assert!(is_palindrome(""));
    assert!(is_palindrome("a"));
    assert!(is_palindrome("A man, a plan, a canal: Panama"));
    assert!(is_palindrome("No 'x' in Nixon"));
    assert!(is_palindrome("Was it a car or a cat I saw?"));
    assert!(!is_palindrome("hello"));
    assert!(!is_palindrome("ab"));
    assert!(is_palindrome("Иди ищи")); // non-ASCII still compared correctly

    println!("palindrome checks passed");
}
```

`flat_map` is required rather than `map` because `char::to_lowercase` returns an iterator: multi-char lowercase expansions like `İ` -> `i` + combining dot are handled. The two-pointer sweep compares from both ends and stops early on the first mismatch, so it is O(n) time and O(n) space for the normalized copy. Punctuation and whitespace are dropped by `is_alphanumeric`.

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <string.h>

void reverse(char *s) {
    if (s == NULL) {
        return;
    }

    char *left = s;
    char *right = s + strlen(s) - 1;

    while (left < right) {
        char tmp = *left;
        *left = *right;
        *right = tmp;
        left++;
        right--;
    }
}

int main(void) {
    char a[] = "hello";
    reverse(a);
    printf("%s\n", a);  /* olleh */

    char b[] = "";
    reverse(b);
    printf("%s\n", b);  /* empty */

    char c[] = "x";
    reverse(c);
    printf("%s\n", c);  /* x */

    reverse(NULL);  /* must not crash */

    char d[] = "ab";
    reverse(d);
    printf("%s\n", d);  /* ba */

    return 0;
}
```

The `NULL` guard keeps the function safe on empty pointers. `strlen(s) - 1` would wrap around for an empty string since `strlen` returns `size_t` and subtracting one underflows; however, because `right` points one element *before* `s`, the comparison `left < right` is immediately false and the loop body never runs, so the empty case is still correct. I used array literals (`char a[] = "hello"`) rather than string literals (`char *s = "hello"`) because string literals live in read-only memory and writing to them is undefined behavior.

