The `fold` lambda adds counts for the first string and subtracts them for the second, so a zeroed table means the letter multisets match. Using `unsigned char` before `isalpha`/`tolower` avoids undefined behaviour on bytes above 127, and punctuation is simply ignored.

```rust
#[derive(Debug)]
pub struct RingBuffer<T> {
    data: Vec<Option<T>>,
    head: usize,
    len: usize,
}

impl<T> RingBuffer<T> {
    pub fn new(capacity: usize) -> Self {
        assert!(capacity > 0, "capacity must be > 0");
        let mut data = Vec::with_capacity(capacity);
        data.resize_with(capacity, || None);
        RingBuffer { data, head: 0, len: 0 }
    }

    pub fn push(&mut self, value: T) {
        let cap = self.data.len();
        let idx = (self.head + self.len) % cap;
        if self.len == cap {
            self.head = (self.head + 1) % cap; // drop oldest
        } else {
            self.len += 1;
        }
        self.data[idx] = Some(value);
    }

    pub fn iter(&self) -> impl Iterator<Item = &T> {
        let cap = self.data.len();
        (0..self.len).map(move |i| self.data[(self.head + i) % cap].as_ref().unwrap())
    }
}

fn main() {
    let mut rb = RingBuffer::new(3);
    for v in [1, 2, 3, 4, 5] {
        rb.push(v);
    }
    let seen: Vec<i32> = rb.iter().copied().collect();
    assert_eq!(seen, vec![3, 4, 5]);
    println!("{:?}", seen);
}
```

`head` marks the oldest slot and `len` counts live elements, so wrapping is pure modular arithmetic. Once full, `head` advances before the write, overwriting the oldest value; `iter` walks the live range in logical order, which the assertion confirms for overflow of `[1,2,3,4,5]` into a size-3 buffer.

