```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <string.h>

static void reverse_in_place(char *s) {
    size_t i = 0;
    size_t j = strlen(s);
    while (i < j) {
        j--;
        if (i >= j) {
            break;
        }
        char tmp = s[i];
        s[i] = s[j];
        s[j] = tmp;
        i++;
    }
}

int main(void) {
    char buf[] = "Hello, world";
    reverse_in_place(buf);
    printf("%s\n", buf);
    return 0;
}
```
The two indices meet in the middle, so each pair is swapped exactly once. The `if (i >= j) break;` guard avoids swapping the middle character with itself when the length is odd. `strlen` is computed once before the loop, and a mutable array `buf[]` is used because string literals are read-only.

