        while (i < n && s[i] == ' ') i++;
        int j = i;
        while (j < n && s[j] != ' ') j++;
        reverse_range(s, i, j - 1);      /* each word back */
        i = j;
    }
}

int main(void) {
    char buf[256] = "  hello   world  from c  ";
    reverse_words(buf);
    printf("[%s]\n", buf);
    return 0;
}
```

The classic two-pass trick: reversing the whole string flips letter order too, so reversing each word again restores letters while keeping the word order reversed. Runs in place, O(1) extra space, and tolerates multiple spaces and leading/trailing ones.

