fn dedup_in_place(items: &mut Vec<i32>) -> usize {
    let mut write = 0;
    for read in 0..items.len() {
        if write == 0 || items[read] != items[write - 1] {
            items[write] = items[read];
            write += 1;
        }
    }
    items.truncate(write);
    write
}

fn main() {
    let mut a = vec![1, 1, 2, 3, 3, 3, 2, 4, 4];
    assert_eq!(dedup_in_place(&mut a), 6);
    assert_eq!(a, vec![1, 2, 3, 2, 4]);

    let mut empty: Vec<i32> = Vec::new();
    assert_eq!(dedup_in_place(&mut empty), 0);

    let mut single = vec![7];
    assert_eq!(dedup_in_place(&mut single), 1);
    assert_eq!(single, vec![7]);
    println!("ok");
}
```

The two-index technique writes kept items back over the front of the same `Vec`, so no second allocation is needed. Only *consecutive* duplicates collapse, which is why `3, 3, 3, 2` keeps a `2` after the run. Compile with `rustc -O dedup.rs` or `cargo run`.

```rust
#[derive(Debug, Clone, Copy)]
enum Op {
    Add(i64),
    Mul(i64),
    Clamp { min: i64, max: i64 },
}

fn apply(ops: &[Op], mut value: i64) -> i64 {
    for op in ops {
        value = match *op {
            Op::Add(n) => value + n,
            Op::Mul(n) => value * n,
            Op::Clamp { min, max } => value.clamp(min, max),
        };
    }
    value
}

fn main() {
    let ops = [
        Op::Add(10),
        Op::Mul(3),
        Op::Clamp { min: 0, max: 100 },
    ];
    assert_eq!(apply(&ops, 5), 45);
    assert_eq!(apply(&ops, 100), 100);
    assert_eq!(apply(&[], 7), 7);
    assert_eq!(apply(&[Op::Mul(2)], 0), 0);
    println!("ok");
}
```

`clamp` panics only if `min > max`, so callers constructing `Clamp` should keep the bounds ordered; for anything untrusted, validate before building the variant. `i64` keeps the multiplication from overflowing for normal inputs, though a debug build will panic on a genuine overflow — switching to `wrapping_mul`/`checked_mul` is the fix if that matters.

