```c
#include <stddef.h>
#include <stdio.h>

int binary_search(const int *arr, size_t n, int target) {
    size_t lo = 0;
    size_t hi = n; /* exclusive upper bound */
    while (lo < hi) {
        size_t mid = lo + (hi - lo) / 2; /* avoids overflow */
        if (arr[mid] == target) {
            return (int)mid;
        } else if (arr[mid] < target) {
            lo = mid + 1;
        } else {
            hi = mid;
        }
    }
    return -1;
}

int main(void) {
    int a[] = {-8, -3, 0, 4, 4, 7, 19, 42};
    size_t n = sizeof a / sizeof a[0];

    printf("%d\n", binary_search(a, n, 4));    /* 3 (first match) */
    printf("%d\n", binary_search(a, n, 42));   /* 7 */
    printf("%d\n", binary_search(a, n, -8));   /* 0 */
    printf("%d\n", binary_search(a, n, 5));    /* -1 */
    printf("%d\n", binary_search(a, n, 100));  /* -1 */
    printf("%d\n", binary_search(NULL, 0, 1)); /* -1 */
    return 0;
}
```

The half-open range `[lo, hi)` removes the off-by-one errors of the classic formulation. `lo + (hi - lo) / 2` cannot overflow even for very large arrays, unlike `(lo + hi) / 2`. With duplicates such as the two `4`s in the sample, the algorithm returns *some* matching index — here index 3 — not necessarily the first or last occurrence. An empty array returns -1 immediately because `lo < hi` is false at once.

```rust
use std::collections::HashMap;

/// Returns the nth Fibonacci number (F(0) = 0, F(1) = 1).
/// Errors if n is too large to fit in u64.
fn fib(n: u32, memo: &mut HashMap<u32, u64>) -> Result<u64, String> {
    if n >= 94 {
        return Err(format!("F({n}) overflows u64 (max safe n is 93)"));
    }
    if let Some(&v) = memo.get(&n) {
        return Ok(v);
    }
    let value = if n < 2 {
        n as u64
    } else {
        fib(n - 1, memo)? + fib(n - 2, memo)?
    };
    memo.insert(n, value);
    Ok(value)
}

fn main() {
    let mut memo = HashMap::new();
    assert_eq!(fib(0, &mut memo).unwrap(), 0);
    assert_eq!(fib(1, &mut memo).unwrap(), 1);
    assert_eq!(fib(10, &mut memo).unwrap(), 55);
    assert_eq!(fib(50, &mut memo).unwrap(), 12586269025);
    assert_eq!(fib(93, &mut memo).unwrap(), 12200160415121876738);
    assert!(fib(94, &mut memo).is_err());
    println!("ok");
}
```

F(93) = 12,200,160,415,121,876,738 is the largest Fibonacci number that fits in `u64`, so `n >= 94` returns an `Err` instead of silently wrapping. The `HashMap` memo makes the recursion linear in `n` rather than exponential. The `?` operator propagates the range error out of the recursive calls. Because the caller owns the mutable map, repeated queries across calls reuse cached values.

