```rust
#[derive(Debug, PartialEq)]
pub enum ParseError {
    Empty,
    MissingUnit,
    BadNumber,
    UnknownUnit(char),
}

pub fn parse_duration(input: &str) -> Result<u64, ParseError> {
    let s = input.trim();
    if s.is_empty() {
        return Err(ParseError::Empty);
    }

    let mut total: u64 = 0;
    let mut digits = String::new();

    for ch in s.chars() {
        if ch.is_ascii_digit() {
            digits.push(ch);
            continue;
        }
        if digits.is_empty() {
            return Err(ParseError::MissingUnit);
        }
        let n: u64 = digits.parse().map_err(|_| ParseError::BadNumber)?;
        digits.clear();
        total += match ch {
            's' => n,
            'm' => n * 60,
            'h' => n * 3600,
            'd' => n * 86_400,
            other => return Err(ParseError::UnknownUnit(other)),
        };
    }

    if !digits.is_empty() {
        return Err(ParseError::MissingUnit);
    }
    Ok(total)
}

fn main() {
    for case in ["1h30m", "45s", "2d", " 90m ", "", "10x", "5"] {
        println!("{case:?} -> {:?}", parse_duration(case));
    }
}
```

Digits accumulate until a unit character arrives, so multi-digit values like `120m` work. Trailing digits with no unit (`"5"`) are rejected instead of silently assumed to be seconds. Shown output: `"1h30m"` -> `Ok(5400)`, `""` -> `Err(Empty)`, `"10x"` -> `Err(UnknownUnit('x'))`, `"5"` -> `Err(MissingUnit)`.

