```c
#include <stddef.h>

void reverse_string(char *s) {
    if (s == NULL) return;
    size_t len = 0;
    while (s[len] != '\0') len++;
    if (len < 2) return;

    size_t i = 0, j = len - 1;
    while (i < j) {
        char tmp = s[i];
        s[i] = s[j];
        s[j] = tmp;
        i++;
        j--;
    }
}
```

Two indices walk toward each other and swap, so it is O(n) time and O(1) space. The `NULL` guard and the `len < 2` early return handle bad pointers, empty strings, and single characters; it works on any byte string, including UTF-8, because it never assumes a character width.

