```python
def longest_palindrome(s: str) -> str:
    if not s:
        return ""

    def expand(left: int, right: int) -> tuple[int, int]:
        while left >= 0 and right < len(s) and s[left] == s[right]:
            left -= 1
            right += 1
        return left + 1, right  # last valid half-open range

    start, end = 0, 0
    for i in range(len(s)):
        l1, r1 = expand(i, i)        # odd-length centers
        l2, r2 = expand(i, i + 1)    # even-length centers
        if r1 - l1 > end - start:
            start, end = l1, r1
        if r2 - l2 > end - start:
            start, end = l2, r2
    return s[start:end]


if __name__ == "__main__":
    cases = ["babad", "cbbd", "", "a", "forgeeksskeegfor", "你好世界好你", "abacabad"]
    for c in cases:
        print(repr(c), "->", repr(longest_palindrome(c)))
```

`expand` walks outward while the two indices stay in bounds and match, then returns the last valid window. Trying both odd (`i, i`) and even (`i, i + 1`) centers covers every palindrome. Empty input returns `""` because the loop never runs; a single character returns itself. Unicode works since Python strings index by code point.

