```cpp
#include <cstdint>
#include <iostream>
#include <utility>

// Returns (F(n), F(n+1)) using fast doubling.
std::pair<std::uint64_t, std::uint64_t> fib_pair(unsigned n) {
    if (n == 0) return {0, 1};
    auto [a, b] = fib_pair(n / 2);
    std::uint64_t c = a * (2 * b - a);   // F(2k)
    std::uint64_t d = a * a + b * b;     // F(2k+1)
    if (n % 2 == 0) return {c, d};
    return {d, c + d};
}

std::uint64_t fib(unsigned n) {
    return fib_pair(n).first;
}

int main() {
    for (unsigned n : {0u, 1u, 2u, 10u, 50u, 90u}) {
        std::cout << "F(" << n << ") = " << fib(n) << '\n';
    }
    return 0;
}
```

Fast doubling derives F(2k) and F(2k+1) from the pair (F(k), F(k+1)), so it only recurses log₂(n) deep — `F(90)` needs about 7 frames. Beware overflow: `uint64_t` holds Fibonacci up to F(93); past that you need a big-integer type. The `n % 2` branch picks the odd or even identity.

