`split_whitespace` ignores runs of spaces and leading/trailing whitespace, and `to_lowercase` makes counting case-insensitive. The comparator sorts count descending then word ascending, so ties are deterministic. Empty input yields an empty `Vec`.

```cpp
#include <vector>
#include <stdexcept>

class EmptyRange : public std::runtime_error {
public:
    EmptyRange() : std::runtime_error("cannot take the minimum of an empty vector") {}
};

template <typename T>
T min_of(const std::vector<T>& values) {
    if (values.empty()) {
        throw EmptyRange();
    }
    T best = values.front();
    for (const auto& v : values) {
        if (v < best) {
            best = v;
        }
    }
    return best;
}
```

A dedicated exception type distinguishes the empty case from unrelated failures. The template works for any type with `operator<`, and starting from `front()` avoids requiring a default-constructible `T`.

