```rust
struct Fib {
    cache: Vec<u64>,
}

impl Fib {
    fn new() -> Self {
        Fib { cache: vec![0, 1] }
    }

    fn get(&mut self, n: usize) -> u64 {
        while self.cache.len() <= n {
            let len = self.cache.len();
            let next = self.cache[len - 1] + self.cache[len - 2];
            self.cache.push(next);
        }
        self.cache[n]
    }
}

fn main() {
    let mut fib = Fib::new();
    for n in [0, 1, 2, 10, 50] {
        println!("fib({n}) = {}", fib.get(n));
    }
}
```

The cache starts seeded with `[0, 1]` so indexing is valid from the first call. `get` extends the vector only as far as needed, giving amortised O(1) per subsequent query and sharing work across calls. `u64` overflows around `fib(93)`, so the loop stops at 50 to stay exact.

