def longest_palindrome(s: str) -> str:
    """Return the longest palindromic substring, expanding around each center."""
    if not s:
        return ""

    start, end = 0, 0  # inclusive bounds of the best palindrome found

    def expand(left: int, right: int):
        while left >= 0 and right < len(s) and s[left] == s[right]:
            left -= 1
            right += 1
        return left + 1, right - 1  # last valid bounds

    for i in range(len(s)):
        l1, r1 = expand(i, i)       # odd-length center
        l2, r2 = expand(i, i + 1)   # even-length center
        if r1 - l1 > end - start:
            start, end = l1, r1
        if r2 - l2 > end - start:
            start, end = l2, r2

    return s[start:end + 1]


if __name__ == "__main__":
    cases = ["babad", "cbbd", "", "a", "forgeeksskeegfor", "abba"]
    for c in cases:
        print(repr(c), "->", repr(longest_palindrome(c)))
```
`expand` walks outward while characters match, then returns the inner last-valid bounds. Using strict `>` keeps the first-found palindrome on ties. The empty string returns `""` immediately. Run time is O(n²) worst case with O(1) extra space.

