```python
def dedupe(items: list) -> list:
    """Order-preserving dedupe that works for hashable and unhashable items."""
    seen = []
    out = []
    for item in items:
        try:
            if item in seen:
                continue
        except TypeError:
            pass  # unhashable in a set-based path, fall back below
        out.append(item)
        seen.append(item)
    return out


if __name__ == "__main__":
    print(dedupe([1, 2, 1, 3, 2]))            # [1, 2, 3]
    print(dedupe([[1], [2], [1]]))            # [[1], [2]]
    print(dedupe([]))                          # []
```

This uses a list for `seen` so lists and other unhashable values work; the membership check is O(n) per item, so for large hashable-only lists a `set` is faster.

