`Result` implements `Sum`, so the iterator collects into a `Result<i32, String>` and stops at the first `Err`. The output is `Ok(12)` for the good slice and `Err("boom")` for the bad one. Double-checking the arithmetic: 1+2+3 doubled is 12, and `2*1 + 2*3` is never computed because the error short-circuits before `Ok(3)` is folded in.

```c
#include <errno.h>
#include <limits.h>
#include <stdbool.h>
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>

bool parse_int(const char *s, int *out) {
    if (s == NULL || *s == '\0' || out == NULL) return false;

    errno = 0;
    char *end = NULL;
    long value = strtol(s, &end, 10);

    if (errno == ERANGE || value < INT_MIN || value > INT_MAX) return false;
    if (end == s || *end != '\0') return false;

    *out = (int)value;
    return true;
}

int main(void) {
    const char *samples[] = {"42", "-7", "2147483648", "12abc", "", " 3"};
    for (int i = 0; i < 6; i++) {
        int v = 0;
        printf("%-12s -> %s", samples[i], parse_int(samples[i], &v) ? "ok " : "bad");
        if (v != 0) printf(" (%d)", v);
        putchar('\n');
    }
    return 0;
}
```
`strtol` does the heavy lifting but reports overflow through `errno`, and `end` must point at the terminator for the whole string to be a valid number. `"2147483648"` exceeds `INT_MAX` on typical 32-bit-int platforms, and `" 3"` is rejected because the leading space means `end != s + consumed` leaves a non-numeric suffix.

```cpp
#include <algorithm>
#include <iostream>
#include <stdexcept>
#include <vector>

template <typename T>
T median(std::vector<T> values) {  // by value: caller's copy is untouched
    if (values.empty()) {
        throw std::invalid_argument("median of empty vector is undefined");
    }
    std::size_t mid = values.size() / 2;
    std::nth_element(values.begin(), values.begin() + mid, values.end());
    if (values.size() % 2 == 1) {
        return values[mid];
    }
    T lower = *std::max_element(values.begin(), values.begin() + mid);
    return (lower + values[mid]) / static_cast<T>(2);
}

int main() {
    std::vector<int> a{7, 1, 3, 9, 5};
    std::vector<int> b{4, 2, 8, 6};
    std::cout << median(a) << '\n';  // 5
    std::cout << median(b) << '\n';  // 5
    std::cout << a.size() << ' ' << b.size() << '\n';  // unchanged
}
```
`nth_element` partitions in O(n) so the odd case returns the middle order statistic directly. For the even case the lower median is the max of the left partition, and the two middles are averaged. Taking the vector by value guarantees the caller's data is untouched; sizes print as `5 4` at the end.

