```python
def longest_palindrome(s: str) -> str:
    """Return the longest palindromic substring of s.

    Expands around each center (2n-1 centers). O(n^2) time, O(1) space.
    Empty input returns "".
    """
    if not s:
        return ""

    start, end = 0, 0

    def expand(left: int, right: int) -> tuple[int, int]:
        while left >= 0 and right < len(s) and s[left] == s[right]:
            left -= 1
            right += 1
        return left + 1, right - 1

    for i in range(len(s)):
        lo, hi = expand(i, i)          # odd length
        if hi - lo > end - start:
            start, end = lo, hi
        lo, hi = expand(i, i + 1)      # even length
        if hi - lo > end - start:
            start, end = lo, hi

    return s[start:end + 1]


if __name__ == "__main__":
    cases = ["babad", "cbbd", "", "a", "forgeeksskeegfor", "\u4f60\u597d\u4f60"]
    for c in cases:
        print(repr(c), "->", repr(longest_palindrome(c)))
```

`"babad"` yields `"bab"`, `"cbbd"` yields `"bb"`, and the empty string returns `""`. The expansion handles single characters and even-length palindromes without special-casing.

