`fib(0) = 0`, `fib(10) = 55`, and `fib(187)` is the last value that fits in `u128` — `fib(188)` exits with the overflow message on stderr. Parsing rejects anything that is not a `u32`.

```cpp
#include <algorithm>
#include <iostream>
#include <optional>
#include <stdexcept>
#include <type_traits>
#include <vector>

template <typename T>
std::enable_if_t<std::is_arithmetic_v<T>, T>
median(std::vector<T> values) {
    if (values.empty()) {
        throw std::invalid_argument("median of empty vector");
    }
    std::sort(values.begin(), values.end());
    const std::size_t n = values.size();
    if (n % 2 == 1) {
        return values[n / 2];
    }
    return static_cast<T>((values[n / 2 - 1] + values[n / 2]) / 2);
}

int main() {
    std::cout << median<int>({3, 1, 4, 1, 5}) << '\n';       // 3
    std::cout << median<double>({4.0, 1.0, 2.0, 3.0}) << '\n'; // 2.5
    std::cout << median<int>({-5, -1, -3}) << '\n';          // -3

    try {
        median<int>({});
    } catch (const std::invalid_argument& e) {
        std::cout << "caught: " << e.what() << '\n';
    }
    return 0;
}
```

The vector is taken by value so the caller's data is not reordered. For even counts the average of the two middle elements is cast back to `T`; for integers that truncates toward zero, which is the usual expectation for `median<int>`. Empty input throws rather than returning a misleading zero.

