Here's a complete C program. It tracks the largest and second-largest **distinct** values, handling duplicates and insufficient input.

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <limits.h>

int main(void) {
    long max1 = LONG_MIN, max2 = LONG_MIN;
    long x;
    int has = 0;

    while (scanf("%ld", &x) == 1) {
        has = 1;
        if (x > max1) {
            max2 = max1;
            max1 = x;
        } else if (x < max1 && x > max2) {
            max2 = x;
        }
    }

    if (!has || max2 == LONG_MIN) {
        printf("Not enough distinct values.\n");
    } else {
        printf("Second largest distinct: %ld\n", max2);
    }
    return 0;
}
```

How it works: `max1`/`max2` start at `LONG_MIN`. A new value strictly greater than `max1` demotes the old `max1` to `max2`; a value strictly between them upgrades `max2`. Duplicates of `max1` are ignored (the `x < max1` guard), so only **distinct** values count. `scanf` returning non-1 (EOF or bad input) ends the loop.

Edge cases: empty input and single-value input both print the fallback message since `max2` stays at `LONG_MIN`; negative-only input works because we use `LONG_MIN`, not `0`, as the sentinel. Note `LONG_MIN` is a valid input, so if a user literally enters it as the only second value the fallback triggers — for full correctness swap in a `have_max2` flag.

This uses `std::istringstream` for splitting, which respects the locale's whitespace and leaves multi-byte UTF-8 sequences untouched since it operates on `char` boundaries.

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <sstream>
#include <string>

int main() {
    std::string line;
    while (std::getline(std::cin, line)) {
        std::istringstream iss(line);
        std::string token;
        while (iss >> token) {
            std::cout << token << '\n';
        }
    }
    return 0;
}
```

Why this works: `operator>>` on a stream skips leading whitespace (spaces, tabs, newlines within the line), then reads until the next whitespace, collapsing runs of separators automatically. Because it never splits a `char`, a UTF-8 codepoint like `é` (two bytes) stays contiguous — splitting on whitespace can never cut a multi-byte sequence, though you should still treat tokens as raw bytes.

Edge cases: an empty line yields no tokens; leading/trailing whitespace is ignored; input with only whitespace prints nothing. If you need to preserve empty fields (e.g. CSV-style), `getline(iss, token, delim)` is the right tool instead — but for whitespace tokenisation this is the cleanest idiom.

Checked arithmetic gives us overflow detection for free, so no panics and a clean `Option` return.

```rust
fn factorial(n: u64) -> Option<u64> {
    let mut acc: u64 = 1;
    for i in 2..=n {
        acc = acc.checked_mul(i)?;
    }
    Some(acc)
}

fn main() {
    assert_eq!(factorial(0), Some(1));
    assert_eq!(factorial(1), Some(1));
    assert_eq!(factorial(5), Some(120));
    assert_eq!(factorial(20), Some(2432902008176640000));
    assert_eq!(factorial(21), None); // overflows u64
    assert_eq!(factorial(100), None);
    println!("all checks passed");
}
```

Key points: the `?` operator on `checked_mul` short-circuits and returns `None` the moment a product exceeds `u64::MAX`. `0..=n` is empty when `n == 0`, so `0! == 1` falls out naturally without a special case. `20!` is the largest factorial that fits in a `u64` (it's ≈ 2.43e18, under the 1.8e19 limit), and `21!` exceeds it — the assertions above confirm both boundaries.

If you wanted big values instead, return `num_bigint::BigUint`; for a bounded-domain API the `Option` is the honest contract.

