    while (x) {
        x &= x - 1;
        count++;
    }
    return count;
}
```

Subtracting 1 flips the lowest set bit and all bits below it, so `x & (x - 1)` clears exactly one set bit per iteration. The loop runs once per set bit rather than once per bit position, so it is fast for sparse values and still correct for `x = 0` (returns 0).

