#include <iostream>
#include <vector>
#include <cstdint>

uint64_t fib(int n) {
    if (n < 0) return 0;
    std::vector<uint64_t> memo(n + 1, 0);
    for (int i = 0; i <= n; i++) {
        if (i == 0) {
            memo[i] = 0;
        } else if (i == 1) {
            memo[i] = 1;
        } else {
            memo[i] = memo[i - 1] + memo[i - 2];
        }
    }
    return memo[n];
}

int main() {
    for (int n : {0, 1, 10, 50, 93}) {
        std::cout << n << " -> " << fib(n) << '\n';
    }
    return 0;
}
```

The bottom-up table avoids recursion depth and recomputation; `memo[i]` depends only on the two previous entries, so a full table is fine but a two-variable rolling form would use less memory. `uint64_t` holds values through `fib(93)`, which is the largest term that fits in 64 bits; beyond that it wraps, so callers needing larger n should use a big-integer type.

