fn is_valid_ipv4(s: &str) -> bool {
    let parts: Vec<&str> = s.split('.').collect();
    if parts.len() != 4 {
        return false;
    }

    for part in parts {
        if part.is_empty() || part.len() > 3 {
            return false;
        }
        if !part.bytes().all(|b| b.is_ascii_digit()) {
            return false;
        }
        // Reject leading zeros like "01", but allow a single "0".
        if part.len() > 1 && part.starts_with('0') {
            return false;
        }
        match part.parse::<u16>() {
            Ok(n) if n <= 255 => {}
            _ => return false,
        }
    }
    true
}

fn main() {
    let cases = [
        ("192.168.0.1", true),
        ("0.0.0.0", true),
        ("255.255.255.255", true),
        ("256.1.1.1", false),
        ("1.2.3", false),
        ("1.2.3.4.5", false),
        ("01.2.3.4", false),
        ("1.2.3.", false),
        ("1.2.3.a", false),
    ];
    for (s, want) in cases {
        assert_eq!(is_valid_ipv4(s), want, "failed for {s}");
    }
    println!("all cases passed");
}
```

Splitting on `.` and requiring exactly four parts rejects both too-short and too-long strings. The digit and length checks run before `parse` so things like `"+1"` or a 4-digit octet can't slip through. Parsing as `u16` and bounding at 255 handles the range check without a separate overflow path, and the leading-zero rule matches dotted-quad expectations for sockets and configs.

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stddef.h>

int binary_search(const int *arr, size_t n, int target) {
    if (arr == NULL || n == 0) return -1;

    size_t lo = 0;
    size_t hi = n;          /* exclusive upper bound */

    while (lo < hi) {
        size_t mid = lo + (hi - lo) / 2;  /* avoids overflow */
        if (arr[mid] < target) {
            lo = mid + 1;
        } else if (arr[mid] > target) {
            hi = mid;
        } else {
            return (int)mid;   /* any matching index is acceptable */
        }
    }
    return -1;
}

int main(void) {
    int a[] = {1, 3, 3, 5, 7, 9, 11};
    size_t n = sizeof a / sizeof a[0];

    printf("%d\n", binary_search(a, n, 3));   /* 1 or 2 */
    printf("%d\n", binary_search(a, n, 1));   /* 0 */
    printf("%d\n", binary_search(a, n, 11));  /* 6 */
    printf("%d\n", binary_search(a, n, 4));   /* -1 */
    printf("%d\n", binary_search(a, 0, 4));   /* -1 */
    printf("%d\n", binary_search(NULL, 0, 4));/* -1 */
    return 0;
}
```

The half-open bound `[lo, hi)` keeps the loop invariant simple: `arr[lo..hi)` is the only region that can still contain the target. `mid = lo + (hi - lo) / 2` cannot overflow even on the largest `size_t`, unlike `(lo + hi) / 2`. With duplicates the function returns whichever match the search lands on, not necessarily the first, which is the standard behaviour and worth documenting for callers.

