A function is meant to reverse a string but returns garbage on an empty string and on a single-character string. Here it is:

char *reverse(char *s) {
    char *out = malloc(strlen(s) + 1);
    int n = strlen(s);
    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) out[i] = s[n - 1 - i];
    out[n] = '\0';
    return out;
}
What is wrong, and fix it.

Two problems, one of them the real cause of the garbage:

1. `malloc(strlen(s) + 1)` does not null-terminate: the buffer is filled but `out[n] = '\0'` writes the terminator one past the allocated block (need `+1` for the terminator, which we do have — but the loop copies exactly n bytes, so out[n] is the terminator; the allocation is correct). The visible failure is actually that `out` is never filled when the loop does not run.

Wait — the loop never runs for n = 0, and out[0] = '\0' is set, so empty string is fine. For n = 1 the loop runs once (out[0] = s[0]) and out[1] = '\0' is set, so a single char is also fine. The real hazard is the missing `#include <string.h>` and, more importantly, the missing `malloc` failure check. The reversal itself is correct.

Fixed version with the missing check:

#include <string.h>
#include <stdlib.h>

char *reverse(const char *s) {
    size_t n = strlen(s);
    char *out = malloc(n + 1);
    if (out == NULL) return NULL;
    for (size_t i = 0; i < n; i++) out[i] = s[n - 1 - i];
    out[n] = '\0';
    return out;
}

