Explain why this loop never terminates:

int i = 0;
while (i < 10) {
    printf("%d\n", i);
}

The loop body never changes `i`. The condition `i < 10` is re-evaluated each iteration, but since nothing inside the body mutates `i`, it stays at its initial value forever, so the condition stays true and the loop spins indefinitely printing `0`.

The fix is to increment `i` inside the body:

```c
int i = 0;
while (i < 10) {
    printf("%d\n", i);
    i += 1;
}
```

Check: with `i += 1`, the sequence is 0,1,2,...,9, after which i becomes 10 and the condition `10 < 10` is false — termination. Without it, the value of `i` is invariant at 0, and `0 < 10` never becomes false, so no exit.

