Is this Rust function guaranteed to terminate? Explain.

```rust
fn count_down(mut n: i32) {
    while n != 0 {
        n -= 1;
    }
}
```

No, not as written. If `n` is negative, `n -= 1` moves it further from zero, so `n != 0` stays true forever — e.g. `count_down(-1)` loops indefinitely (… → -2 → -3 …).

It terminates for n ≥ 0 (decrements toward 0) but overflows for very large n in debug builds. A safe version checks the direction:

```rust
fn count_down(mut n: i64) {
    while n > 0 {
        n -= 1;
    }
}
```

